Move a .env into a secret manager: print the commands, or upload to GitHub.

For eight destinations evnx prints the commands for you to run and contacts nothing; only github uploads (over the GitHub API).

Usage: evnx migrate [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --from <FROM>
          Source system: `env-file` or `environment`
          
          Without --from, evnx asks in a terminal and uses `env-file` without one. `environment` requires `--include`: a shell's or CI job's environment holds tokens you did not mean to send. evnx lists the names it will send.
          
          [possible values: env-file, environment]

      --to <TO>
          Destination system: github | aws-secrets-manager | doppler | infisical | gcp-secret-manager | azure-keyvault | vercel | heroku | railway
          
          [possible values: github, aws-secrets-manager, doppler, infisical, gcp-secret-manager, azure-keyvault, vercel, heroku, railway]

  -n, --dry-run
          Preview what would be migrated without making any changes

      --print-only
          Write only the commands to stdout, with the real values; everything else goes to stderr.
          
          `evnx migrate --to heroku --heroku-app my-app --print-only | sh` runs them. In a terminal, without --print-only or --output, the values are shown as `<hidden>`; when stdout is not a terminal (a pipe, a file) and neither flag is given, the real values are printed, so pipelines keep working. Not for github, which uploads instead of printing.

  -o, --output <FILE>
          Write the commands, with the real values, to FILE (mode 0600).
          
          `evnx migrate --to gcp-secret-manager --project p -o upload.sh` saves a script you can read, then run. An existing FILE is refused without --force. Not for github, which uploads instead of printing.

      --force
          Replace FILE when it already exists (with --output)

      --gitignore
          Add the --output file to .gitignore without asking (no prompt)

      --no-gitignore
          Skip the .gitignore check for the --output file

      --skip-existing
          Skip secrets that already exist at the destination

      --overwrite
          Overwrite secrets that already exist without prompting

      --include <INCLUDE>
          Comma-separated glob patterns — only migrate matching keys. Example: --include "DB_*,AWS_*"

      --exclude <EXCLUDE>
          Comma-separated glob patterns — skip matching keys. Example: --exclude "*_LOCAL,*_TEST"

      --strip-prefix <STRIP_PREFIX>
          Strip this prefix from key names before uploading. Example: --strip-prefix "APP_"  →  APP_DB_URL becomes DB_URL

      --add-prefix <ADD_PREFIX>
          Add this prefix to key names before uploading. Example: --add-prefix "PROD_"

      --allow-collisions
          When two names become one, keep the last value.
          
          `--strip-prefix` and `--add-prefix` can merge names (`API_X` and `X` under `--strip-prefix API_`), and Azure Key Vault and GitHub ignore case. Without this flag that is an error naming both, exit 2.

      --repo <REPO>
          GitHub repository in owner/repo format

      --secret-name <SECRET_NAME>
          AWS Secrets Manager secret name, e.g. prod/myapp/config

      --aws-profile <AWS_PROFILE>
          AWS CLI named profile

      --project <PROJECT>
          Doppler project, Infisical project ID, or GCP project ID (required for gcp-secret-manager)

      --doppler-config <DOPPLER_CONFIG>
          Doppler config name (dev / staging / prd)

      --infisical-env <INFISICAL_ENV>
          Infisical environment name (dev / staging / prod)

      --vault-name <VAULT_NAME>
          Azure Key Vault name

      --heroku-app <HEROKU_APP>
          Heroku application name

      --vercel-project <VERCEL_PROJECT>
          Vercel project ID or name (default: the linked project)

      --railway-project <RAILWAY_PROJECT>
          Railway project ID (default: the linked project). Needs --railway-environment, as Railway does

      --railway-environment <RAILWAY_ENVIRONMENT>
          Railway environment, e.g. production

      --railway-service <RAILWAY_SERVICE>
          Railway service (default: the linked service, or the project's only one)

      --no-color
          Disable colored output

  -q, --quiet
          Print nothing on success. Findings, warnings, errors and requested data still print

  -v, --verbose
          Enable verbose output

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

  -V, --version
          Print version

Files:
      --env <FILE>
          The .env file to read (default: .env, or `[defaults] env_name`)

      --env-name <NAME>
          Read `.env.<NAME>` instead of `.env`.
          
          `--env-name production` reads `.env.production`. A name whose file does not exist is an error listing the environments that do. Without it, `[defaults] env_name` in `.evnx.toml` applies, as for the other commands.

Confirmation:
  -y, --yes
          Upload without asking "Proceed with migration?" (github).
          
          For CI. Without a terminal and without `--yes` the upload stops before contacting GitHub (exit 4). ⚠️ It does not answer "overwrite?": a secret that already exists still needs `--overwrite` or `--skip-existing`.

Examples:
  # Print the commands, read them, then run them
  evnx migrate --to heroku --heroku-app my-app

  # Save the commands as a private script (0600), values included
  evnx migrate --to gcp-secret-manager --project my-gcp-project -o upload.sh

  # Run them straight away
  evnx migrate --to heroku --heroku-app my-app --print-only | sh

  # Another environment: .env.production
  evnx migrate --env-name production --to azure-keyvault --vault-name prod-vault

  # Upload to GitHub Actions from CI (token from GITHUB_TOKEN)
  evnx migrate --to github --repo owner/repo --yes --skip-existing

  # Preview: no commands printed, nothing uploaded
  evnx migrate --to doppler --project myapp --doppler-config dev --dry-run

Destinations:
  Prints commands: aws-secrets-manager (--secret-name), gcp-secret-manager (--project),
                   azure-keyvault (--vault-name), heroku (--heroku-app), vercel,
                   doppler, infisical, railway
  Uploads:         github (--repo; GITHUB_TOKEN)

TLS (GitHub upload): evnx trusts its bundled root certificates plus any in the PEM bundle
named by SSL_CERT_FILE or the directory named by SSL_CERT_DIR. Behind a TLS-inspecting
proxy, set SSL_CERT_FILE to your organisation's CA bundle.

Exit codes:
  0  Ran, nothing to report
  1  Ran, and found something: findings, problems, a difference, not in sync
  2  Could not run: usage, config, I/O, or refused before any change
  4  Declined: answered no, or a confirmation was needed with no terminal and no --yes
  141  Output closed early (broken pipe)

📖  Full guide: https://docs.evnx.dev/cli/commands/migrate
